Bounded model checking of kernel code paths with CBMC.

A second engine runs the same target directories natively: native.sh
builds harness, kernel code and models with clang, sanitizers and
libFuzzer, and draws every nondet value from the fuzzer's input.  It
cross-checks the models, runs real sizes and loop counts, and with
NATIVE_SAN=msan checks that copyout never sees uninitialised kernel
memory.  See native/README.native.

	sh native.sh usb/usps 30	# RESULT: CLEAN | FINDING | VACUOUS | ERROR

Needs cbmc (tested with 6.8.0; written against 5.95.1, see "CBMC
versions") and gcc on the build host.  The kernel
sources are preprocessed with a minimal stand-in for a compile directory,
so no config(8) run is needed.

	sh cbmc.sh usb/usps

and ends with a RESULT line; see "Reading the output".

CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR=<dir> replaces kernel files by same-named files in <dir>,
to run a target against an older version of a file.

Environment
	CBMC_TIMEOUT=<s>	limit of each cbmc run, default 300
	CBMC_MEMLIMIT_MB=<n>	address space limit of each cbmc run, default
				6000; see "Reading the output", OUT OF MEMORY
	CBMC_BUDGET_FILE, CBMC_BUDGET
				run budget, see "Run budget"
	CBMC_NO_COVER=1		skip the reachability run (diagnosis only)
	CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR=<dir>	see above
	CBMC_DRY=1		build the model, apply loop contracts, list
				the loops, stop; not counted in the budget
	<target>/timeout	default CBMC_TIMEOUT of that target
	CBMC_WORKDIR=<dir>	keep intermediate files and cbmc output there
	CBMC_TRACE=1		on VIOLATED, one more cbmc run writes a compact
				counterexample to <workdir>/trace.out and prints
				the values assigned from main on (scalars and
				fields; bytes written through a cast pointer,
				such as a sockaddr laid over a buffer, are only
				in trace.out) and the violated property; the
				trace is of the first failed property of the
				check run; trace.out goes on with traces of
				no-body markers, so read its first "Trace for"
				block.  Check that the traced property is in
				kernel code or a postcondition, not an
				assertion on the state the harness built (one
				trace ended on the harness's own precondition).
				The printed summary is cut at 60 lines; with a
				large struct (tcpcb) grep trace.out for the
				inputs.
	CBMC_KEEP=1		keep the temporary work directory also when the
				result is VERIFIED; without CBMC_WORKDIR the
				directory is removed on exit after a VERIFIED
				run and kept otherwise, "output in" says which

Reading the output
	A run prints, in this order: "run N of BUDGET" (only with a budget
	file), the PHASE line of the cover run (with its HINT lines), UNREACHED
	lines for must_reach anchors that were not reached, the failed
	properties of the check run, the PHASE line of the check run (with
	its HINT lines), the NOBODY line, "output in <dir>" and the RESULT
	line, which is always the last line.  A run that stops early prints
	fewer of these.

	PHASE cover  time=21s symex=0.8s vars=93412 clauses=441764 status=ok
	PHASE check  time=14s symex=1.1s vars=307014 clauses=1736131 status=ok failed=0
		time is wall clock seconds of the cbmc run; symex is CBMC's
		"Runtime Symex" (symbolic execution, "-" if it did not
		finish); vars and clauses are the largest SAT problem of any
		solver iteration ("N variables, M clauses"; 0 if none was
		built); status is ok, timeout, oom (see below) or error;
		failed is the number of failed properties of the check run.
		The cover run reports no failed count.

	HINT lines appear when a run timed out or ran out of memory, or has
	more than 1,000,000 variables, and only those that apply:
		- symex is most of the time (or did not finish): the loop
		  bounds are too large.  The loops CBMC unwound most often are
		  listed with their counts; bound them with --unwindset
		  <loop id>:N in opts, or make the model's sizes smaller.
		- many variables, small symex time: the solver is the cost,
		  usually untyped allocations; see "Adding a target", step 3.
		- neither: split the target at an internal interface
		  (assume/guarantee split).

	NOBODY f g h ...
		every function the check run called that has no body
		(CBMC 6's reachable "no body for callee" properties, or
		5.95's "no body for function" warnings), sorted, or
		"NOBODY -".  Each of them returned an arbitrary value and
		had no side effects: no memory was read, written or checked.
		That is intended for hardware access and for subsystems a
		target leaves out (splraise, pool_wakeup, printf, ...) and
		a model gap for anything else: a string function, a
		copy, a lock that protects state the target checks.  Read
		it on every new target.  Longer than about 1000 bytes it is
		cut with "... (N more)".

	RESULT line and exit status:
		RESULT: VERIFIED			0
		RESULT: VIOLATED			1
		RESULT: VACUOUS				2  a must_reach anchor
							   was not reached
		RESULT: UNQUALIFIED (...)		2  CBMC_NO_COVER and no
							   failure
		RESULT: TIMEOUT (coverage run)		2  cbmc ran longer than
		RESULT: TIMEOUT				2  CBMC_TIMEOUT seconds
		RESULT: OUT OF MEMORY (cover)		2  cbmc hit the memory
		RESULT: OUT OF MEMORY (check)		2  limit or was killed
		RESULT: ERROR (coverage run, cbmc exit N) 2  tool failure
		RESULT: ERROR (cbmc exit N)		2
		RESULT: ERROR (reason)			2  configuration error:
							   usage, missing target
							   directory or file,
							   name in keep and
							   nobody, must_reach
							   anchor for an unknown
							   file (checked before
							   any work is done)
		RESULT: BUDGET EXHAUSTED (n runs used)	3  nothing was run
	A build failure (goto-cc, goto-instrument) prints its messages and
	exits 2 without a RESULT line.

	Each cbmc run is limited with ulimit -v to CBMC_MEMLIMIT_MB, so that
	a runaway solver fails by itself with "Out of memory" and not by the
	kernel's OOM killer taking other processes down.  Raise the limit
	only after the HINT lines have been tried.

	If cbmc.sh is interrupted or killed with SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP
	it stops its cbmc process first.  SIGKILL cannot be caught: after a
	kill -9 of the script the cbmc may still be running.

CBMC versions
	The targets, the expect files and this README were written against
	CBMC 5.95.1.  CBMC 6 differs in three ways that cbmc.sh hides:
	- it enables checks by default (malloc may fail, signed overflow,
	  undefined shifts, pointer primitives, bounds); cbmc.sh passes
	  --no-standard-checks, so a run checks what it checked under
	  5.95 (see "What is modelled" and the opts of each target).
	  M_WAITOK allocations still never fail.
	- it prints less: cbmc.sh passes --verbosity 8, which brings back
	  the Runtime Symex, variables and "Unwinding loop" lines the
	  PHASE and HINT lines are made from.
	- a call to a function without a body becomes a property
	  "[F.no-body.G] ... no body for callee G", FAILURE when the call
	  is reachable.  cbmc.sh moves the raw output to check.raw, builds
	  the NOBODY line from these properties, and leaves them out of
	  check.out, the failure list and the counts.
	- --pointer-check does not check array indexing (a[i] on a local,
	  global or member array); cbmc.sh adds --bounds-check.  Results
	  before 2026-11-24 were obtained without it; with it the selftest
	  and every target gave the same results.
	CBMC 6's library has a vsnprintf body that writes up to size bytes
	in a loop; cbmc.sh bounds it with --unwindset vsnprintf.1:17
	(mbinit's 16 byte pool names) before the target's opts, so a
	target can raise it (usb/usps: 33, for the 32 byte sensor
	descriptions).  The model writes and checks the buffer, which
	5.95's did not.
	netinet/tcp_dooptions (704,000 variables) passed within its 600 s
	limit under 5.95; under 6.8 the default solver did not finish in
	1500 s and --sat-solver cadical takes about 1100 s, so the target
	now has that option and a 1500 s timeout.  Other targets took
	about as long as before.

Running the suite
	sh suite.sh [-j N] [-n | -m] [target-dir ...]

	Runs every target (every directory with a harness.c, except
	selftest/, which has its own runner), or only the named ones, and compares each RESULT word with the target's own
	files:
		expect		first line: the expected RESULT word of cbmc.sh
				(VERIFIED, VIOLATED, ...).  A target without it
				is not run and counts as a failure.
		timeout		optional: seconds, exported as CBMC_TIMEOUT for
				that target (default 300).  Record it here when
				the check phase needs more than the default.
		expect.native	optional: the expected RESULT word of native.sh
				(CLEAN, FINDING, VACUOUS).
	-j N runs N jobs at a time (default 1): each target run and each
	mutant run is one job (xargs -P), targets with a timeout file first.
	The report is printed at the end, in the order of -j 1; meanwhile
	one "done <name> <result>" line per job goes to stderr.  The floor
	is the longest single run (net/pfsync_input: cover and check about
	8 minutes each quiet, 23 minutes together under load; the
	tcp_sack_holes and tcp_dooptions runs 10 to 18).  Memory: each cbmc
	run may use CBMC_MEMLIMIT_MB (6000); most use far less, but N
	should not exceed the memory divided by about 4 GB.  Timings under
	-j are 1.5 to 2 times the quiet ones; timeout files have that
	margin.  CBMC is single-threaded, so -j is the only use of more
	cores.  OpenBSD's make regress does not help: bsd.regress.mk and
	bsd.subdir.mk run their targets in a shell loop even under make -j.
	-m also runs every target on each of its mutant*/ directories
	(kernel files with a defect put back) and expects VIOLATED: the
	mutation checks are part of the suite.
	-n runs native.sh (default duration) instead of cbmc.sh, and compares
	with expect.native, which is then required; the engines are not run
	together.  Output is one line per target,
		PASS|FAIL target result expected [check=Ns vars=N]
	(check time and variables from the PHASE check line), the tail of the
	output of a failed run, and "suite: N passed, M failed, S s".  Exit
	status is non-zero if anything failed.  SUITE_LOGDIR=<dir> keeps the
	complete output of every target.  Change a target's expect file only
	together with the change that explains the new result: a shared model
	that turns VERIFIED into VACUOUS shows up here.

Run budget
	With CBMC_BUDGET_FILE=<path> every invocation appends one line to the
	file: start time (epoch seconds), target, final RESULT and wall
	clock seconds, tab separated.  Before doing anything the script
	counts the lines in the file; if the count is CBMC_BUDGET (default
	20) or more it prints "RESULT: BUDGET EXHAUSTED (n runs used)" and
	exits 3.  Otherwise it prints "run N of BUDGET".  Interrupted and
	failed runs are logged too ("ERROR (no result, exit N)").
	A run killed from outside (an outer timeout(1), a background job
	limit) is such a run and counts: do not wrap cbmc.sh in timeout,
	use CBMC_TIMEOUT or the target's timeout file.
	A run longer than the agent tool's foreground limit (about 2
	minutes) must be started with the tool's background option and
	polled; starting it with & is against the rules.  Background
	commands are stopped after 30 minutes unless the call gives a
	longer limit: suite.sh -m on a target whose check takes 10
	minutes needs about an hour (each mutant is a full check).
	An orchestrator that gives a run budget to a harness author picks a
	path for the file, passes CBMC_BUDGET_FILE and CBMC_BUDGET in the
	task description (the author exports them for every cbmc.sh call)
	and reads the file afterwards to see what was run and how long it
	took.  The check is only as good as the rule that all runs go
	through cbmc.sh: it does not stop anyone from calling cbmc,
	goto-cc or a private copy of the script directly.  See "Rules for a
	harness".

What is modelled
	- real bodies of the functions listed in <target>/keep, for files in
	  <target>/sources
	- all functions of files the harness #includes (<target>/included):
	  they keep their bodies, "keep" does not apply to them; so do
	  inline functions defined in headers
	- functions in <target>/nobody lose their body wherever they are
	  defined, also in an included file or a header, and return any
	  value with no side effects.  A name in both keep and nobody is an
	  error.
	- every other function loses its body
	- malloc(9)/free(9) map to CBMC's heap (common/stubs_alloc.c);
	  M_NOWAIT allocations may fail.  free(9) checks its size argument
	  as kern_malloc.c does under DIAGNOSTIC: a size that is non-zero and
	  larger than the allocation's bucket (the allocation size rounded
	  up to a power of two, at least 16, or to whole pages above two
	  pages) is "free: size too large"; a size of half the bucket or
	  less, for a bucket above 16 bytes, is "free: size too small"; size
	  0 is accepted.  The allocation size is that of the object, also
	  for objects made with CBMC_NEW.  pool(9) sits on top of that
	  (common/stubs_pool.c); the harness sets pr_size, also when it
	  replaces pool_get: natively the common pool_get runs regardless.
	  replace does not apply natively to any function common/ defines
	  (pool_get, pool_put): a model that counts calls must keep its
	  count assertions under #ifndef NATIVE_SHIM_H (netinet/
	  tcp_sack_holes), or the harness defines pool_get as a macro for
	  its model after #include <sys/pool.h> (netinet/tcp_reass).
	  Otherwise the native run reports a false FINDING.
	- KASSERT (DIAGNOSTIC is defined): goto-cc turns its __assert call
	  into an assertion, so a failing KASSERT is reported; unlike
	  panic it does not end the path (selftest kassert_fails).
	- bcopy is a byte loop; targets that reach it bound it with
	  --unwindset __builtin_bcopy.0:N
	- memcpy and memmove are CBMC's models, which check both regions
	- copyin, copyout and copyinstr (common/stubs_user.c): user memory
	  is not an object of the model.  A user address is never
	  dereferenced, each copyin yields fresh arbitrary bytes, any copy
	  may fail, and the kernel side must be valid for the full length.
	  This covers bad lengths, direct dereference of a user pointer
	  and double fetches; it does not cover copying uninitialised
	  kernel memory out.
	- <target>/stubflags may select -DMEMCPY_HAVOC: memcpy checks both
	  regions but stores arbitrary bytes.  Use only where nothing
	  depends on the copied values.
	- -DMEMCPY_TRACK (stubflags): memcpy and memmove check both regions,
	  store nothing, and follow one byte the harness names (common/
	  mbufkit.h, mbufkit_track): the model keeps the locations that hold
	  a copy of it, and a content check is a pointer comparison.  For
	  data inside structures (mbufs): every byte written at a symbolic
	  offset into a struct costs thousands of SAT variables and turns
	  later field reads into byte operations; CBMC's memmove into one
	  mbuf alone was 3.1 million variables.  Values code reads after a
	  copy are then not the source's; see "Writing a packet parser
	  target".  Natively the copy is real.
	- -DMEMCPY_BYTELOOP (stubflags): memcpy and memmove are byte loops
	  (--unwindset __builtin_memcpy.0:N, __builtin_memmove.0:N and .1:N);
	  about 7000 variables per byte into an mbuf.
	- libkern (common/stubs_libkern.c, linked through stubs_alloc.c):
	  bzero, explicit_bzero, bcmp, memcmp, timingsafe_bcmp, memchr,
	  strnlen, strlcpy, strlcat, ffs, fls, flsl had no body (sys/systm.h
	  maps bzero, bcmp and memcmp to compiler builtins; CBMC's library
	  has none of the others): they returned arbitrary values and
	  touched no memory.  The models do what sys/lib/libkern does,
	  byte by byte, so an over-read or overflow is reported; loops are
	  bounded with --unwindset NAME.0:N (list in the file).  memset,
	  strlen, strcmp, strncmp, strchr, strrchr, strncpy and strncasecmp
	  are CBMC's.  strnstr, scanc and skpc have no model.  Natively the
	  builtins are the C library's.
	- CBMC_NEW and CBMC_NEW_ARRAY objects are visible to
	  --memory-leak-check (they were not before: __CPROVER_allocate
	  bypasses CBMC's leak bookkeeping; selftest mbufkit_new_leak).
	- panic(9) ends the path and is reported as "panic reached";
	  malloc(9) panics on requests above its size limit unless
	  M_CANFAIL is given
	- <target>/replace redirects calls to a kernel function to a model
	  in the harness
	- <target>/fptargets binds calls through function pointers, so a
	  call through a freed object's method table is reported as a bad
	  dereference but not followed

Known limits of the checker
	- CBMC treats ptr->field through a pointer to a union as an access
	  to the whole union.  Code that overlays a union on variable-length
	  records is reported whenever a record is shorter than the union,
	  even if the field is in bounds.  acpi/madt_validate allocates
	  slack after the table to avoid this, and says what that hides.
	- A walk over records at offsets that depend on the data is
	  expensive.  acpimadt_attach as a whole (three walks plus a
	  16-iteration interrupt loop) did not finish in ten minutes;
	  check the validator and the consumers separately.
	- The common malloc and pool models allocate untyped byte arrays;
	  see "Adding a target", step 3, for what that costs and what to do.
	- CBMC_NO_COVER=1 skips the reachability run to diagnose a slow
	  target.  The result is then UNQUALIFIED, never VERIFIED.
	- Sequence-number arithmetic (TCP): a symbolic base for the
	  comparisons the property makes is cheap where the code
	  compares against it a few times (tcp_sack_option: snd_una), and
	  timed out where every block is compared with it and with every
	  other block (tcp_update_sack_list: rcv_nxt, 250 s against 11 s
	  for a constant).  A list in a symbolic order timed out in every
	  form tried there (swap networks, rotations, one swap).  State
	  properties on small offsets from the base, place the wrap and
	  zero inside the data with a constant base, and list what that
	  leaves out.
	- Cover and check can differ by orders of magnitude (1 s against
	  194 s with one variable made symbolic).  The cover run also
	  obeys CBMC_TIMEOUT.

What is not modelled
	- interrupts, other threads, locks
	- anything a target's harness comment lists as an assumed contract

Adding a target
	1. Copy the closest existing target directory.
	2. harness.c: build the objects the entry point expects, call it,
	   then call the function that tears the objects down.  To reach
	   static functions, #include the kernel file and list it in
	   "included" instead of "sources".  Include it also when the
	   harness needs the file's macros or the headers it includes
	   (sototcpcb in netinet/tcp_pulloutofband).  A file in "sources"
	   is compiled separately and gets its own copies of header static
	   inline functions (renamed name$link1); nobody cannot name those
	   copies, so a statistics helper such as counters_inc keeps its
	   body there and dereferences a per-CPU array nobody allocated.
	   If such a function fails in a sources file, include the file
	   instead (tcp_subr.c in netinet/tcp_reass).
	3. Allocate objects the code reaches through tables, lists or any
	   pointer that may refer to one of several objects with
	   CBMC_NEW(type) and CBMC_NEW_ARRAY(type, n) from
	   common/cbmc_harness.h (#include "cbmc_harness.h"; it also
	   declares the nondet_* functions).  They give zeroed objects with
	   their real type.  The common malloc and pool models allocate
	   untyped byte arrays, and a field access through a pointer that
	   may refer to several of those is encoded byte by byte: sysv/shm
	   had 2.4 million SAT variables and timed out with pool items from
	   the common pool model, and solves in seconds with items from
	   CBMC_NEW in a pool_get model of its own (replace file:
	   pool_get:model_pool_get).  Objects behind a single pointer, such
	   as a softc, need no such care.  Worked examples: sysv/shm
	   (pool items, handle placed behind the segment) and sysv/semop
	   (tables, sets, undo structures).  Do this from the start; if a
	   target is slow, check it first (HINT lines).
	   CBMC_NEW objects are zero under CBMC and filled with 0xbe
	   natively: set every field the code reads, not only the ones
	   the code never sets (m_next, m_nextpkt).  --memory-leak-check
	   is not a cbmc.sh default; a target that wants it lists it in
	   opts, and then every CBMC_NEW object the harness does not free
	   is a leak.  Make scaffolding the code never frees (a socket, an
	   inpcb, a tcpcb) static, or free it at the end of main.
	4. sources, keep: the files and the functions on the path.
	5. must_reach: anchors on the path under test and in the teardown.
	   An anchor is FILE:TEXT and is reached when any line containing
	   TEXT (a fixed string) is.  Use text every mutant keeps: VACUOUS
	   is decided before VIOLATED, so an anchor a mutant removes hides
	   its failure.  Use the first line of a statement: coverage
	   counts a multi-line condition on its first line only.
	6. opts: every loop on the path needs a bound at least as large as
	   its trip count; --unwinding-assertions reports one that is not.
	   Take loop ids from CBMC_DRY=1: they are not numbered in source
	   order (tcp_sack_option.0 is the inner hole walk, .1 the outer
	   block loop).
	7. Mutants: mutant*/ directories next to the harness, each holding
	   a copy of one kernel file under its own name with one check
	   weakened (by one where possible: deleting a block can leave a
	   redundant later check doing the work); mutant_prefix*/ hold the
	   source before a fix.  suite.sh -m runs each with
	   CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR and CBMC_NO_COVER and expects VIOLATED.
	   A mutant that fails nothing prints UNQUALIFIED (no cover run,
	   no failure) and suite.sh reports FAIL: the change is
	   equivalent under the harness's properties.  Either add the
	   property that distinguishes it, or drop the mutant and name
	   the change in the README entry as not detected (netinet/
	   tcp_reass: SEQ_GT -> SEQ_GEQ).  To show that a panic is
	   reachable only when a caller's guarantee is broken, use a
	   mutant that moves the bound by one (netinet/tcp_pulloutofband,
	   mutant_cnt: "panic reached").  A mutant of an included kernel
	   file is a full copy of that file.
	8. Check degenerate inputs the caller admits before narrowing the
	   input: a length of 0, equal endpoints, an empty list.  tcp_input
	   calls tcp_update_sack_list for a FIN without data, so the
	   segment [s, s) is a real input (FINDINGS).
	A function the code calls from a file the target does not
	compile has no body and returns an arbitrary value: give it a
	model when it returns a pointer or fills an out-parameter
	(in6_lookupmulti, if_get).  A function of an included file that
	must not run needs a replace model, not nobody.  Struct copies and
	compares of 16 bytes (in6_addr assignment, IN6_ARE_ADDR_EQUAL)
	need --unwindset __builtin_bcopy.0:17 (memcmp, bcmp likewise).
	HINT lines on a passing run are information, not a failure.
	Every file in step 4 must exist, empty if unused (an included-only
	target has empty sources and keep).  Run cbmc.sh and suite.sh from
	this directory, with target paths relative to it.

Techniques used by the targets
	- validator, then consumer (acpi/madt_validate, ffs/mountfs)
	- assume/guarantee split: one target asserts a contract at an
	  internal interface, another replaces the callee by a model that
	  assumes it (ipmi/transport_bt and ioctl/ipmi)
	- postconditions as __CPROVER_assert in the harness
	- termination through unwinding assertions
	- ioctl dispatch: arbitrary cmd, buffer of IOCPARM_LEN(cmd) bytes
	- sleep points as interleaving points: tsleep is replaced by a model
	  of what other processes may do meanwhile, using the kernel's own
	  statements for it (sysv/semop removes the semaphore set)
	- mutation check: run a target against a copy of the source with a
	  validation removed (CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR) and expect VIOLATED
	- invariant preservation (netinet/tcp_reass, tcp_sack_holes,
	  tcp_update_sack_list): build a state that satisfies a stated
	  invariant by arithmetic (no __CPROVER_assume), assert the
	  invariant on it (this checks the construction), call the
	  function, assert the invariant and a reference result (count,
	  hull, which elements survive unchanged).  A loop that applies
	  the same step per item (SACK blocks) is covered by one step
	  from any state the invariant allows, when the full loop costs
	  too much; say so in the entry.
	- conservation: one arbitrary position or byte is followed through
	  the call (mbufkit_track): before the cut it stays, after it it
	  moves by the cut's length, and a sequence number held before is
	  held exactly once after (netinet/tcp_pulloutofband, tcp_reass)

Rules for a harness
	- no __CPROVER_assume; bound unconstrained values with arithmetic
	- do not drop functions on the path from keep, or add them to
	  nobody, to make a failure go away
	- when the model lives in a different file than its callers, the
	  harness includes the same headers as the kernel file, otherwise
	  struct types do not match at link time
	- a VERIFIED result counts only together with must_reach anchors
	  on the path under test
	- sizes are small constants; say in the harness what they stand for
	- environment variables do not persist between separate shell
	  invocations: give CBMC_BUDGET_FILE and CBMC_BUDGET on every
	  cbmc.sh and suite.sh command line
	- run the solver through cbmc.sh only: no direct calls of cbmc,
	  goto-cc or goto-instrument for checking, no private copy of the
	  script.  The time, memory and run limits and the budget log exist
	  only there.  Stay within the run budget given in the task.
	- no process may be left running when you stop: no background cbmc
	  runs, no runs started with & or nohup.  Check with ps before you
	  report, and stop what is yours.

The mbuf kit (common/mbufkit.h)
	Targets for network code run the kernel's own mbuf library
	(kern/uipc_mbuf.c, kern/uipc_mbuf2.c: m_get, m_gethdr, m_clget,
	m_free, m_freem, m_extfree, m_extref, m_pullup, m_pulldown,
	m_copydata, m_adj, m_split, ...) on chains the kit builds.  Below
	the mbuf code: pool_get is mbufkit_pool_get (replace file), which
	returns typed objects (an mbuf is a struct mbuf, a cluster a byte
	array of the pool's item size, m_ext_refs a typed mirror), and
	pool_put is mbufkit_pool_put (CBMC's free; the free(9) size check
	of the common pool model only cost time).  pool_init records item
	sizes, so the real mbinit() sets everything up; refcnt_* are
	single-threaded models; NPF is 0, no tags, no M_ZEROIZE.
	PR_NOWAIT/M_DONTWAIT allocations may fail (mbufkit_nofail = 1 turns
	that off; mbufkit_nfail counts failures, MBUFKIT_FAILED()).

	Real allocation code or models: the kit also has models of m_get,
	m_gethdr, m_clget and m_free (replace m_get:mbufkit_m_get, ...).
	Measured on net/mbuf_pullup (2 segments, len up to MHLEN + 8; an
	earlier revision of the kit, before the pool_put model):
	real functions 557,812 variables, check 107 s; models 410,569
	variables, check 75 s, but symex 16.7 s against 2.7 s.  The real
	code is used by every target: it is affordable, and the paths the
	parsers exercise are the kernel's.  The models remain for targets
	where the real m_free's call through mextfree_fns is too costly.

	What made targets tractable (each measured, see the targets):
	  - MEMCPY_TRACK (above): with CBMC's memcpy one copy into an mbuf
	    was 3.1 million variables; with a byte loop 7000 per byte;
	    with tracking a few pointer comparisons.  The length bound
	    then hardly matters (m_pullup with len up to 40 or 152: same
	    formula).
	  - two segments: m_pullup with three did not finish in 150 s, with
	    two it takes about 75 s.
	  - no symbolic modulo: mbufkit_upto(n) clamps instead of
	    nondet % (n + 1), a divider in the formula.
	  - mbufs zeroed by mbufkit_pool_get (real items have arbitrary
	    content: pointer fields read on paths symex cannot rule out
	    then point anywhere, and m_split ran out of memory).  Reads of
	    fields the code never set see 0; natively ASan's fill byte or
	    MSan covers those.
	  - --unwindset m_tag_delete_chain.1:0 and m_tag_copy_chain.2:0
	    (no tags; the unwinding assertion proves the loops are not
	    entered), fptargets binding m_extfree's call.
	  - shared clusters (m_extref) remain expensive: m_split of a
	    cluster exploded even with one segment and is covered only
	    natively.

	API (all in the header, part of harness.c; see the comments there)
	  MBUFKIT_MAXSEG (2)	most mbufs the builder chains
	  MBUFKIT_MAXLEN	largest len passed to the builder; set it to
				the harness bound (> MHLEN forces a cluster for
				a segment that does not fit)
	  MBUFKIT_MAXCHAIN	longest chain the checks walk (MAXSEG + 2);
				--unwind must exceed it
	  MBUFKIT_MEMTRACK 1	with stubflags -DMEMCPY_TRACK
	  MBUFKIT_REFS 1	only with the m_free model and shared clusters
	  mbufkit_init()	first: runs mbinit()
	  mbufkit_build(len, flags)
				len arbitrary bytes, 1..MAXSEG mbufs, arbitrary
				m_len (zero allowed), arbitrary leading space,
				M_PKTHDR and m_pkthdr.len on the first;
				MBUFKIT_CLUSTER: a segment may be a cluster,
				MBUFKIT_RDONLY: a cluster may be read-only,
				MBUFKIT_ANY both, MBUFKIT_PLAIN neither
	  mbufkit_upto(n)	arbitrary int in [0, n]
	  mbufkit_check(m)	well-formedness, asserted (storage bounds,
				M_PKTHDR only first, m_pkthdr.len = sum of
				m_len, M_EXT fields, no cycle within MAXCHAIN);
				mbufkit_check_chain(m, flags) with
				MBUFKIT_CHK_NOHDR, MBUFKIT_CHK_HDR2
	  mbufkit_track(&b, m), mbufkit_track_off(&b, m, off),
	  mbufkit_track_buf(&b, buf, len)
				follow one byte; mbufkit_same(&b, p) says
				whether p holds it; mbufkit_ptr(m, off) is the
				address of packet byte off
	  mbufkit_len, mbufkit_count, mbufkit_byte
	  mbufkit_free(m)	m_freem, so that --memory-leak-check holds
	Selftests mbufkit_*: the builder reaches one segment, a split
	inside the first 4 bytes, clusters, read-only clusters, an empty
	first mbuf and leading space; the checker rejects a wrong
	m_pkthdr.len and data past the storage; a dropped chain is a
	leak.

Writing a packet parser target
	For "f(struct mbuf *m, int off, ...) parses a packet": copy
	net/mbuf_example_parser.
	  harness.c	#define MAXLEN, MBUFKIT_MAXLEN; #include "mbufkit.h";
			then the kernel file (#include "foo.c", listed in
			"included") or nothing (f in "sources")
			main: mbufkit_init(); m = mbufkit_build(
			mbufkit_upto(MAXLEN), MBUFKIT_ANY); off =
			mbufkit_upto(...); call f the way its caller does;
			if f may free or replace m, take the new pointer;
			if m survives: mbufkit_check(m); mbufkit_free(m)
	  sources	kern/uipc_mbuf.c, kern/uipc_mbuf2.c (+ f's file)
	  keep		the list in net/mbuf_example_parser/keep, plus the
			mbuf functions f calls (m_pullup, m_pulldown,
			m_copydata, m_getptr, m_adj, m_dup1, ...) and f
	  replace	pool_get:mbufkit_pool_get
			pool_put:mbufkit_pool_put
	  config	pf.h NPF 0
	  fptargets	m_extfree.function_pointer_call.1/m_extfree_pool
	  stubflags	-DMEMCPY_TRACK
	  opts		--unwind 5, the --unwindset lines of the example,
			--memory-leak-check, bounds for f's own loops
	MEMCPY_TRACK means bytes f gets through m_copydata or reads after
	m_pullup are arbitrary values, not the packet's: fine for a parser
	that reads each byte once.  If f validates a byte and later reads it
	again through another copy, that can give a false VIOLATED; use
	-DMEMCPY_BYTELOOP with a small MAXLEN then (about 7000 variables
	per byte copied into an mbuf).  Check the NOBODY line.
	Cost measured (check phase, 4-core host shared with other runs; the
	cover phase is shorter): the kit alone (mbufkit_ok: build, track,
	check, free) about 100,000 variables; net/mbuf_copydata 141,000 /
	25 s; mbuf_adj 283,000 / 22 s; mbuf_split (internal mbufs only)
	257,000 / 24 s; mbuf_pullup 426,000 / 111 s; mbuf_pulldown
	1,000,000 / 83 s; the example 431,000 / 54 s.  Symbolic execution
	is about 15 s of each.  A third segment took m_pullup past 150 s;
	the length bound costs little with MEMCPY_TRACK; clusters that end
	up shared (m_extref) are the most expensive thing measured.
	Start with MBUFKIT_MAXSEG 2 and MAXLEN just above the largest
	header f pulls up (above MHLEN only if a branch needs it); split a
	target by argument range or by property when it is slow
	(net/mbuf_pulldown and its _prefix, _range, _tail).
	Mutation check: put a copy of f's file with a check removed in a
	directory and run with CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR (the example's mutant/).

When a run exhausts time or memory

	Read the PHASE line first; the remedy depends on where the cost is.
	Measured on netinet/ip_dooptions (40 option bytes), which ran out
	of memory on the real mbuf code:

	1. Out of memory with vars=0 after a few seconds of symex: look for
	   an object of symbolic size (__CPROVER_allocate(n, ...) with an
	   arbitrary n) that is read through a struct pointer.  Allocate a
	   constant size and place the data at the END of the object, so
	   that an overrun is still out of bounds (mc_seg in
	   common/mbufcontract.h).  4 option bytes: out of memory -> 7 s.

	2. Symex dominated by library code below the function under test:
	   prove the function against contract models of the library
	   instead (assume/guarantee).  For mbufs: include
	   common/mbufcontract.h instead of mbufkit.h and leave
	   kern/uipc_mbuf*.c out of sources.  The models state what
	   m_pullup, m_pulldown, m_copydata, m_adj and m_freem promise;
	   the net/mbuf_* targets are the proofs of the real functions.
	   Pointers kept across m_pullup are freed by the model, data past
	   m_len is out of bounds.

	3. Cost grows with the iterations of a loop in the function under
	   test (the HINT names it; time and vars multiply with the
	   bound): give the loop a contract in <target>/loops and it is
	   checked as ONE arbitrary iteration.  40 iterations: out of
	   memory -> 39 s.  The file is the JSON of goto-instrument
	   --loop-contracts-file:

		{ "functions": [ { "ip_dooptions": [ {
		    "loop_id": "0",
		    "invariants": "cnt >= 0 && cnt <= 40 && cp + cnt == g_end",
		    "decreases": "cnt",
		    "assigns": "cnt, cp, ..., __CPROVER_object_whole(g_end)"
		} ] } ] }

	   Recipe:
	   - loop_id is the N of the loop's name FUNCTION.N.
	     CBMC_DRY=1 sh cbmc.sh <target> lists every loop as
	     "LOOP FUNCTION.N file:line"; do { } while (0) macros count
	     as loops (inner first), so pick the real loop by file:line.
	     A mutant that deletes a statement containing such a macro
	     renumbers the ids and the loops file no longer applies: make
	     the check ineffective instead (if (0 && off + 2 > end),
	     pf/pf_walk_header).  The "Unwinding loop" lines of
	     cbmc count loops of inlined callees under the caller and do
	     not give the id.
	   - Check every change to loops, replace, keep or the harness
	     with CBMC_DRY=1 first: it builds the model and applies the
	     contracts, shows configuration errors, runs no check and is
	     not counted in the run budget.
	   - invariants: what ties the loop's cursor to the end of the
	     data (pointer + remaining == end).  State the end as a ghost
	     global that the harness sets (g_end); the loop may write the
	     packet, so do not read the end from the packet.
	   - decreases: an int expression that gets smaller every
	     iteration; this replaces the unwinding assertion.
	   - symbol_map: leave it out.  cbmc.sh (loopmap.py) maps every
	     local and parameter named in the clauses.
	   - assigns: start with the locals the loop writes and
	     __CPROVER_object_whole(<pointer into the packet>).  For a
	     struct the loop writes only in part, name the fields
	     (pd->badopts): object_whole havocs the rest.  Run; every
	     "Check that X is assignable: FAILURE" names a location that
	     is missing; add it and run again until none is left.  Leave
	     the clause out and uninitialised pointer locals make the
	     checks fail.
	   - In a clause use builtin C type names only (unsigned char,
	     not u_char: a typedef name crashes goto-instrument without a
	     message), and no __CPROVER_pointer_object or
	     __CPROVER_pointer_offset (they become bodiless calls, show
	     up under NOBODY and constrain nothing).
	   - A pointer the loop writes (an output field) cannot be bounded
	     with < or > in the invariant: the havocked pointer may be in
	     another object.  State it as equalities with the ghost end
	     pointer (p == 0 || p == g_end - 8 || ...);
	     netinet6/nd6_options generates such a clause.
	   - "loop invariant is preserved" gives no trace.  Find the
	     failing conjunct by removing conjuncts one at a time.
	   - The clauses are parsed as plain C expressions: no macros.
	     Write m->m_hdr.mh_len for m_len, 0 for NULL, numbers for
	     flags.
	   - A loop inside a function called from the contracted loop is
	     refused ("Inner loop ... unwind it first"); opts cannot help,
	     contracts are applied before unwinding; a do { } while (0)
	     macro counts as a loop.  The models of mbufcontract.h are
	     loop-free for this reason; replace any other looping callee
	     by a loop-free model in the harness.
	   - If the loop can free or write an object through a pointer
	     (the mbuf, via icmp6_error or m_freem), assigns needs
	     __CPROVER_object_whole() of it, and the invariant must then
	     restate every field of it that the loop leaves alone and the
	     code after the loop relies on; otherwise that code runs on
	     an arbitrary object (netinet6/ip6_hopopts/loops).
	   - A loops file can only be checked by running cbmc.sh.
	   - Remove the loop's --unwindset from opts.
	   - "Check that loop invariant is preserved: FAILURE" on the
	     unmodified source means the invariant is too weak or wrong,
	     not that the kernel is: strengthen it.  With all contract
	     checks passing, other failures are findings as usual.
	   - The mutation check is mandatory for a target with loops: a
	     removed length check must give VIOLATED (ip_dooptions/mutant).
	   Everything after the loop is checked from an arbitrary state
	   that satisfies the invariant and the negated loop condition.
	   native.sh ignores the file and runs the real loop.

	   A loop contract does not fit, and the fallback is a bounded
	   --unwindset on a small packet, when
	   - the loop body allocates: assigns cannot name objects that do
	     not exist before the loop (the m_pulldown model in the tag
	     loop of net/pppoe_disc; 26-byte packets, check 250 s);
	   - the invariant would have to describe data not yet visited
	     (a second pass over an option chain that a first pass
	     validated: net/sppp_lcp_rcr; 24-byte packets, check 15 s).
	   An empty loops file means no contracts.

	4. Strings that are not compile-time constants through the
	   byte-loop libkern models: one strlcpy of a 64 byte buffer with
	   a single symbolic byte cost 24,500 variables.  Give strings
	   constant shapes per branch (memset, then one character per if
	   arm), or replace the string functions by loop-free region
	   models with the real loops under NATIVE_SHIM_H (pf/pf_addrule).

	5. A queue.h or tree.h macro loop without its own bound unwinds
	   on every explored path: TAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE over three entries
	   unwound 280 times and the cover run timed out; --unwindset on
	   its id (from CBMC_DRY, inner loops first) brought it to 35 s
	   (pf/pf_addrule).  Count the "Unwinding loop" lines per id in
	   check.out before splitting a target.

	6. Integer-to-pointer casts case-split over every object at each
	   cast: kern/subr_tree.c's rb_n2e/rb_e2n (node to entry through
	   unsigned long) ran symex out of memory.  Replace them by models
	   doing the same arithmetic on char pointers (pf/pf_sourcelim_add).
	   A target over an RBT tree also needs kern/subr_tree.c in
	   sources, _rb_insert/_rb_remove and their helpers and the tree's
	   compare function in keep, fptargets for the comparator call, and
	   --unwindset for the _rb_* loops sized to the seeded tree.

	7. The solver dominates (symex small, time in the solver): an
	   external solver is a constant factor only.  net/rtm_xaddrs:
	   default 380 s, --sat-solver cadical 290 s,
	   --external-sat-solver cadical 234 s.  Prefer 2 and 3.

	Splitting into cases by packet length did not help ip_dooptions:
	the single case of 40 option bytes ran out of memory by itself.

When the only failures are pointer arithmetic

	"pointer outside object bounds" or "pointer relation" on an
	expression that is never dereferenced: a bounds test written as
	cursor + n > end forms the pointer before comparing it.  With the
	data at the end of its object every such test is visible, the
	constant ones too (cursor + 4 > end fails once the cursor has
	reached the end), so expect more failures than variable-length
	tests alone.  In the kernel the same pointer leaves its object
	only when the data ends its buffer.  Steps:
	1. Copy the kernel file to a scratch directory and rewrite each
	   failing test as a length comparison (n > end - cursor), nothing
	   else.  Run with CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR on the copy: VERIFIED shows
	   that nothing else is wrong.
	2. Build mutant/ from the rewritten copy with one test removed;
	   it must fail on a dereference.
	3. Leave the tree alone and set expect to the result on the tree
	   (VIOLATED); report the rewrite as a diff.  Whoever applies it
	   to the tree then saves the old source as mutant_prefix/ and
	   sets expect to VERIFIED.
	The native engine does not see this class: CLEAN is expected.
	Worked examples: netinet6/nd6_options, net80211/rsn_parse.

Targets
	usb/usps
		usps_attach followed by usps_detach.  With usbd_open_pipe_intr
		storing *pipe before checking usbd_transfer's result this
		target is VIOLATED (the freed pipe is closed again by
		usps_attach and by usps_detach).
	usb/umcs
		umcs_attach followed by umcs_detach.
	usb/uow
		uow_attach followed by uow_detach.  uow_cmd is not in keep:
		its retry loop waits for a status byte that only the
		interrupt handler updates, so it has no bound in this model.
	usb/ubcmtp
		ubcmtp_setup_pipes followed by ubcmtp_disable on a softc
		built by hand; the softc definition is copied from ubcmtp.c
		and has to be kept in sync with it.
	udf/dirstream
		udf_opendir, up to three udf_getfid calls, udf_closedir, on a
		directory whose extents have arbitrary sizes (0..64 bytes,
		block size 48) and arbitrary content.  udf_readatoffset is
		replaced by a model; assumed contract: on success *data is
		valid for *size bytes.  Checks bounds, buffer release and
		leaks.  VIOLATED before the udf_getfid length checks.
	ffs/mountfs
		ffs_mountfs on a device with arbitrary block contents; bread
		is replaced by a model.  VIOLATED on the current tree: a
		superblock accepted by ffs_validate can still have
		fs_sbsize smaller than struct fs, fs_fsize of zero, an
		fs_cssize that malloc(9) refuses, fs_bsize larger than
		fs_frag * fs_fsize, and an fs_ncg that overflows the size
		computation.  The two loops over fs_cssize and fs_ncg have no
		small bound, so this target cannot reach VERIFIED as is.
	acpi/madt_validate
		acpimadt_validate on an MADT of arbitrary content and length.
		The table copy is exactly hdr.length bytes (acpi_maptable).
		Shows that the walk terminates and stays inside the table
		(up to the slack described above).  VIOLATED before the
		check that two bytes remain for the entry header.
	acpi/amlprims
		aml_parseopcode, aml_parseend and aml_parsesimple started at
		an arbitrary position of a short AML scope.  VIOLATED on the
		current tree: aml_parselength and the word, dword and qword
		cases of aml_parsesimple read past scope->end.
		aml_parseopcode is clean.
	ipmi/transport_bt
		One ipmi_cmd over the BT interface with a BMC that answers
		anything.  Asserts that the reply fits the caller's buffer.
		VIOLATED before ipmi_recvcmd checked the reply length and
		bt_recvmsg used a counter wider than the length byte.
	ipmi/transport_smic
		One ipmi_cmd over the SMIC interface with a BMC that
		answers anything.  Asserts that the reply fits the caller's
		buffer and that nothing is stored in sc_buf beyond the part
		ipmi_recvcmd lends (c_maxrxlen + datarcv); the loops of
		smic_sendmsg and smic_recvmsg terminate (unwinding
		assertions).  VIOLATED before smic_recvmsg bounded its read
		loop by c_maxrxlen (mutant/ipmi.c is that file).  A reply
		longer than the buffer ends the loop and fails on the end
		status like kcs_recvmsg; the model's status reads are
		biased towards 0xc0-0xc7 for the native engine only.
	ioctl/ipmi
		Two arbitrary ioctl calls on /dev/ipmi with ipmi_cmd replaced
		by a model that assumes the contract above.  VIOLATED when
		the data_len check in IPMICTL_SEND_COMMAND is removed.
	sysv/msg_copy
		msg_copyin and msg_copyout for an arbitrary length.  Asserts
		the chain lengths and the returned length.  VIOLATED before
		msg_copyout compared lengths as size_t.
	sysv/shm
		Three arbitrary calls out of shmget, shmat, shmdt and shmctl
		on a table of two segments, then shmexit.  UVM objects are a
		reference-counted model.  VIOLATED when the table bound in
		shm_find_segment_by_shmid is removed, and when
		shm_delete_mapping leaves a freed segment in the table.
	sysv/semop
		One sys_semop with arbitrary arguments on a set of two
		semaphores, at most two operations, one undo entry.  While
		the caller sleeps the set may change or be removed; at most
		two wakeups.  The path that allocates the operation array
		(more than 8 operations) is not covered.  VIOLATED when the
		sem_num check is removed, and when the check that the set
		still exists after the sleep is removed.
	net/mbuf_pullup
		m_pullup(m, len) on a chain of up to 2 mbufs (internal,
		cluster or read-only cluster, arbitrary m_len and leading
		space), packets and len up to MHLEN + 8.  NULL only for a
		short packet or a failed allocation, chain freed then;
		otherwise len bytes contiguous, chain well formed, length
		and content unchanged.  VIOLATED when the alignment slack is
		left out of the new mbuf's size and when the source m_data is
		not advanced.
	net/mbuf_pulldown, _prefix, _range, _tail
		m_pulldown(m, off, len, &off) with off and len up to
		MLEN + 8 (24 for _tail), arguments beyond the packet
		included.  mbuf_pulldown: NULL contract, result on the chain,
		range contiguous at *offp, chain well formed (the M_PKTHDR
		duplicate below tolerated); _prefix, _range, _tail: bytes
		before, in and after the range in place, the range at
		mtod(n) + *offp.  Split four ways for solver time.  VIOLATED
		when the "chain too short" check is off by one and when the
		trailing m_adj is dropped.
	net/mbuf_pulldown_dup1
		m_pulldown(m, 0, len, &off) with the range inside a
		read-only first mbuf: m_dup1 copied the packet header for
		off == 0 and the copy became the second mbuf, with M_PKTHDR
		and a duplicated header.  Caller with off 0: net/bfd.c
		bfd_input.  No memory-safety effect found.  Fixed: m_dup1
		always uses MGET and MLEN (a cluster above that).  VERIFIED;
		mutant/uipc_mbuf2.c is the pre-fix file and keeps the
		finding (CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR=net/mbuf_pulldown_dup1/mutant:
		VIOLATED).
	net/mbuf_copydata
		m_copydata(m, off, len, buf) for every range inside a packet
		of up to 40 bytes into a heap buffer of exactly len bytes.
		VIOLATED when the offset is not reset for the next mbuf.
	net/mbuf_adj
		m_adj(m, req_len), req_len in [-(26), 26] on packets up to 24
		bytes.  Length max(plen - |req_len|, 0), remaining bytes in
		place.  VIOLATED when the head trim subtracts req_len instead
		of what was trimmed, and when the tail trim's count is not
		clamped at 0.
	net/mbuf_split
		m_split(m0, len0, wait): head and tail well formed, lengths
		len0 and plen - len0, concatenation is the packet; NULL leaves
		the chain intact.  CBMC: internal mbufs, packets up to 24
		bytes; natively also clusters (m_extref, reference counts) and
		the recursive case.  VIOLATED when the head keeps its m_len.
	net/mbuf_example_parser
		Worked example for parser targets (see "Writing a packet
		parser target"): a TLV parser using m_copydata and
		m_pullup.  VIOLATED with mutant/tlv.c (length check
		dropped: m_copydata panics).

netinet/ip_dooptions
	ip_dooptions() of sys/netinet/ip_input.c on an arbitrary IP header
	of 6..15 words, on the contract models (common/mbufcontract.h),
	with the option loop under a loop contract (loops).  VERIFIED:
	no out-of-bounds access, the walk ends (decreases clause), the
	chain is intact on return 0.  mutant/ (optlen > cnt removed) is
	VIOLATED.  About 75 s.

netinet6/ip6_hopopts
	ip6_process_hopopts() of sys/netinet6/ip6_input.c on an arbitrary
	option area of 0..32 bytes behind an IPv6 header, contract models,
	loop contract.  VERIFIED.  mutant_prefix/ is the source before
	the icmp6_error offset arguments were reordered ("erroff + opt +
	k - opthead" formed the pointer erroff + opt, which can lie past
	the mbuf's data): VIOLATED.  mutant/ (PadN length check removed)
	is VIOLATED with an out-of-bounds read of opt[1].  Native: CLEAN.

netinet6/ip6_hopopts_input
	ip6_hopopts_input() on packets of up to 56 bytes with
	ip6_process_hopopts replaced by a model asserting what the
	target above assumes: the option bytes are readable and
	contiguous within one mbuf's m_len.  VERIFIED, about 100 s;
	mutant/ (second header fetch too short) is VIOLATED.

netinet6/nd6_options
	nd6_options() of sys/netinet6/nd6.c on an arbitrary flat option
	area of 0..64 bytes, loop contract.  VERIFIED, with the
	postcondition that every pointer left in *ndopts is an option
	inside the area.  mutant_prefix/ is the source that formed
	nd_opt + olen before the bounds test (a pointer up to 2040 bytes
	past the area, then compared): VIOLATED.  mutant/ (zero-length
	check removed) is VIOLATED on the decreases clause.  Native:
